{
  "teamId": 133,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Oakland Athletics entered the season with high hopes but stumbled badly out of the gate, going a pedestrian 11-15 in April as their bullpen — anchored by Blake Treinen, Fernando Rodney, and Joakim Soria — proved dangerously unreliable. Late-game leads evaporated with alarming regularity, and the A's found themselves buried in the AL West standings before May had even arrived. It was a brutal opening chapter for a team that had surprised the baseball world just a year prior.\n\nThen something clicked. Beginning in May and accelerating through June, July, and August, Oakland caught fire in a way few teams in baseball could match. The offense — powered by 257 home runs and an .775 OPS — provided run support in waves, while the rotation and a retooled bullpen tightened up. Matt Olson, Matt Chapman, Marcus Semien, Ramon Laureano, and Khris Davis took turns delivering clutch hits and towering home runs. The A's went 16-10 in May, 17-11 in June, 15-9 in July, and a scorching 17-9 in August, climbing inexorably up the wild card standings. Even when opposing hitters found gaps or bullpen arms faltered in high-leverage moments, Oakland kept winning — grinding through adversity with a resilience that defined the franchise.\n\nSeptember brought the finishing kick. The A's went 19-8 over the final month, their best record of the year, culminating in Matt Chapman's 35th home run and a 97-win season that secured their Wild Card berth. It was a remarkable turnaround from those dark April days, a testament to the depth and determination of Bob Melvin's roster. The late-season surge felt like destiny building toward a postseason run.\n\nBut October proved unforgiving, as it so often does in the one-game Wild Card format. Facing the Tampa Bay Rays in a winner-take-all contest, Sean Manaea gave up home runs to Yandy Diaz and Avisail Garcia while the Oakland offense mustered little in response. The A's fell silent when it mattered most, and a 97-win season ended in a single evening — another chapter in Oakland's complicated postseason history, full of regular-season brilliance and October heartbreak.",
